{
 "set": "clipper-listening",
 "description": "m39-ac, the .clipper insert. Every variant plays the IDENTICAL staccato pluck riff used by the transient-shaper demos, and every CLIPPED variant uses the IDENTICAL settings (driveDb 18, ceilingDb -6, hard mode, outputDb -9).\n\nTHE ONE AXIS IS `oversampling`, and it runs 1 -> 2 -> 4. Nothing else differs, not even the output level: clipping to the same ceiling at the same drive lands all three within ~0.1 LUFS of each other, so this is a fair A/B by construction rather than by trimming.\n\nWHAT TO LISTEN FOR: the clipped tone itself is the same in all three. What changes is a thin metallic FIZZ sitting on top of the notes -- harmonics generated above Nyquist that fold back as inharmonic tones. At 1x it is unfiltered and most audible; at 4x it is ~91 dB down and should be gone. HOW BIG IS IT, MEASURED: the 1x and 4x renders differ by -43.85 dB RMS (2x differs by -59.02 dB), and that difference sits 22 dB higher up the spectrum than the music does, so it is high-frequency fizz rather than a level or tone change. -44 dB is REAL but SUBTLE -- on a laptop speaker it may well be inaudible. If 1x and 4x sound the same to you, that is a real and useful answer, not a failure to listen: it means the oversampling is costing CPU for something you cannot hear on this material, and the default should be reconsidered. The 6272 Hz test tone in `clipper-physics` is where the same defect is 91 dB and unmissable; music hides it, which is exactly why the gate does not use music.\n\nThe control is the bare riff so you can hear what the clipper is adding in the first place.",
